WEBVTT

00:00:04.600 --> 00:00:05.695
Vectors.

00:00:06.015 --> 00:00:08.246
A list of numbers is a place.

00:00:08.846 --> 00:00:10.762
I run the dock at Night School.

00:00:11.082 --> 00:00:14.156
Every crate that comes in gets an address on my chart.

00:00:15.356 --> 00:00:16.219
Here is a crate.

00:00:16.539 --> 00:00:20.707
It sits four squares east of the corner and one and a half squares north.

00:00:21.307 --> 00:00:25.328
Write that down as a list: four, then one and a half.

00:00:25.648 --> 00:00:27.480
That list is a vector.

00:00:28.080 --> 00:00:32.143
You can read it as a place, or as an arrow from the corner to the crate.

00:00:32.463 --> 00:00:34.694
Same list, same thing.

00:00:35.894 --> 00:00:37.284
Arrows add.

00:00:37.604 --> 00:00:40.993
Walk the first one, then walk the second from where you stopped.

00:00:41.313 --> 00:00:46.450
Where you end up is the sum, and its numbers are the two lists added place by place.

00:00:47.050 --> 00:00:48.650
Arrows stretch.

00:00:48.970 --> 00:00:52.044
Times two keeps the direction and doubles the length.

00:00:52.364 --> 00:00:55.690
Times minus one turns it round to face the other way.

00:00:56.890 --> 00:00:58.974
Now two crates, and a game.

00:00:59.294 --> 00:01:03.863
Multiply their lists number by number, and add it all up.

00:01:04.463 --> 00:01:07.537
I'll swing the second crate round the corner while the sum keeps count.

00:01:08.136 --> 00:01:11.800
As the second crate swings round, where will the sum hit zero?

00:01:14.300 --> 00:01:15.542
Right there.

00:01:15.862 --> 00:01:18.135
At a right angle, the sum is zero.

00:01:18.455 --> 00:01:20.455
That sum is called the dot product.

00:01:20.775 --> 00:01:27.638
It is big when two arrows point the same way, zero when they meet at a right angle, and below zero when they point apart.

00:01:28.838 --> 00:01:30.481
The dot product has a catch.

00:01:30.801 --> 00:01:35.495
Watch what happens if I push the second crate twice as far out, in the same direction.

00:01:36.095 --> 00:01:37.927
What happens to the dot product?

00:01:40.427 --> 00:01:41.564
It doubles.

00:01:41.884 --> 00:01:44.810
Nothing about the direction changed, but the score did.

00:01:45.410 --> 00:01:47.473
So divide by the two lengths.

00:01:47.793 --> 00:01:50.130
What is left depends only on the angle.

00:01:50.450 --> 00:01:58.197
It is the cosine of the angle between the arrows: one when they point the same way, zero at a right angle, minus one when they point opposite.

00:01:58.517 --> 00:02:01.191
It always stays between minus one and one.

00:02:02.391 --> 00:02:04.959
My chart has two directions, east and north.

00:02:05.279 --> 00:02:07.553
A list can be as long as you like.

00:02:08.153 --> 00:02:13.774
In the little language model from the attention lesson, every word starts as a list of 32 numbers.

00:02:14.094 --> 00:02:18.389
That makes each word a place in a space with 32 directions.

00:02:18.989 --> 00:02:21.494
Nobody can picture 32 directions.

00:02:21.814 --> 00:02:24.067
The arithmetic doesn't care.

00:02:24.387 --> 00:02:30.787
The dot product is still multiply and add, now 32 times over, and the cosine is still a cosine.

00:02:31.987 --> 00:02:33.987
Nobody set those numbers by hand.

00:02:34.307 --> 00:02:39.401
Training moved them, a little at a time, until the model got better at guessing the next word.

00:02:40.001 --> 00:02:42.338
So look at who ended up close to whom.

00:02:42.658 --> 00:02:46.490
The nearest neighbours of kettle are map, shell and crate.

00:02:46.810 --> 00:02:50.010
Tallow's are rerun, pylon and map.

00:02:50.610 --> 00:03:01.515
Across all 46 words, 80 percent have a nearest neighbour of the same kind as themselves: names next to names, things next to things, doing words next to doing words.

00:03:01.835 --> 00:03:06.151
Shuffle which word owns which list, and it drops to about 23 percent.

00:03:06.471 --> 00:03:08.702
Before training it was 9 percent.

00:03:09.302 --> 00:03:13.829
Trained on hundreds of millions of words, the same idea goes much further.

00:03:14.149 --> 00:03:24.085
In 2013, researchers reported word lists where king, minus man, plus woman, landed closest to queen, once the three words in the question were left out of the search.

00:03:25.285 --> 00:03:26.780
And then there's my cone.

00:03:27.380 --> 00:03:28.622
A cone is cargo.

00:03:28.942 --> 00:03:30.416
It should sit with the crates.

00:03:30.736 --> 00:03:33.115
Its nearest neighbours are who, stood and mended.

00:03:33.715 --> 00:03:39.041
In our sentences a cone only ever turns up in one phrase: put a cone on.

00:03:39.361 --> 00:03:44.161
Nothing there marks it as cargo, so the model had no reason to park it with the crates.

00:03:44.481 --> 00:03:46.944
Where it did park it is something we measured.

00:03:47.264 --> 00:03:49.475
Why there, we can only guess.

00:03:50.075 --> 00:03:55.675
Closeness in these lists comes from how words are used in the training sentences, not from what they mean.

00:03:55.995 --> 00:04:00.858
Here, with 46 words, you can read every sentence the model learned from.

00:04:02.058 --> 00:04:04.942
One warning about pictures, this lesson's included.

00:04:05.542 --> 00:04:12.173
To lay the words out on my flat chart, I squash 32 directions down to the two that keep the most spread.

00:04:12.493 --> 00:04:15.336
Those two keep 31 percent of it.

00:04:15.936 --> 00:04:20.567
On the flat chart, only 52 percent of the words sit nearest to a word of the same kind.

00:04:20.887 --> 00:04:23.519
In all 32 directions it was 80.

00:04:23.839 --> 00:04:26.197
Flattening throws a lot of closeness away.

00:04:27.397 --> 00:04:29.144
A list of numbers is a place.

00:04:29.464 --> 00:04:36.622
The dot product mixes how much two places point the same way with how far out they are, and the cosine keeps only the direction.

00:04:37.222 --> 00:04:39.580
Next along the coast is my crane.

00:04:39.900 --> 00:04:44.342
It moves every place on the chart at once, and that is a matrix.

00:04:44.662 --> 00:04:45.378
Goodnight.

